Claims identified & fact-check:

Framing claim: These are signs of "High Functioning ADHD" (implying a distinct recognized ADHD subtype/category)
→ Unsupported — "High Functioning ADHD" is not a diagnostic subtype in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11 (ICD-11 recognizes only predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, and combined presentations); using this label materially misrepresents ADHD's actual classification system, even though it's a popular informal term
Claim 1 (Sign 5): Regularly losing important items
→ Supported — ICD-11 explicitly lists "losing things" as a core inattention symptom
Claim 2 (Sign 4): Constantly interrupting others mid-conversation
→ Supported — ICD-11 lists "interrupting or intruding on others' conversations or games" as a core impulsivity symptom
Claim 3 (Sign 3): Having a hard time sitting still
→ Supported — ICD-11 lists "difficulty sitting still without fidgeting" / "sense of discomfort with being quiet or sitting still" as a core hyperactivity symptom
Claim 4 (Sign 2): Getting easily distracted during tedious tasks
→ Supported — ICD-11 lists "having difficulty sustaining attention on tasks that do not provide a high level of stimulation or reward" and "being easily distracted by extraneous stimuli" as core inattention symptoms
Claim 5 (Sign 1): Starting many tasks but leaving them unfinished
→ Supported — consistent with ICD-11 inattention symptoms of difficulty completing tasks and organizing activities

